          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Euclid</strong><br>\nNot much is known about the early life of Euclid, as there are no extant records from his time. He lived around 300 BC, and it is believed he was born in Alexandria, Egypt. Alexandria was a hub of learning and culture, with the famous Library of Alexandria situated there. This environment would have been influential in Euclid's education and mathematical works.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Euclid's Major Work</strong><br>\nEuclid's most famous contribution is his work \"Elements,\" which is a collection of books covering a comprehensive system of mathematics. \"Elements\" includes definitions, postulates, and mathematical proofs, and it remains a foundational text in the field of geometry. This work compiled knowledge from earlier mathematicians as well as original work by Euclid himself.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Method of Teaching</strong><br>\nEuclid's work is noted for its methodical and logical approach to mathematics. He used an axiomatic system, beginning with basic premises known as postulates and moving systematically to more complex conclusions. This method not only taught geometry but also encouraged a logical way of thinking, which could be applied beyond mathematics.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
