          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Influence on Mathematics</strong><br>\nElements was used as a textbook for teaching mathematics for over two thousand years. Euclid's approach to geometry and his method of logical deduction were revolutionary. His work laid the groundwork for future mathematicians and is often cited as a prime example of classical Greek mathematical genius.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Five Postulates</strong><br>\nIn \"Elements,\" Euclid begins with five postulates that are assumed to be true without proof. These include statements like \"a straight line can be drawn between any two points.\" These postulates form the basis for all the propositions that follow in his works, setting the standards for deductive reasoning.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Euclid's Other Works</strong><br>\nBesides \"Elements,\" Euclid wrote several other works, including \"Data,\" \"Optics,\" \"Phaenomena,\" and \"On Divisions.\" These dealt with different aspects of mathematics and science, like perspective, conic sections, and number theory, showing his wide range of interests and expertise.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
