          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Geometry before Euclid</strong><br>\nBefore Euclid, other Greek mathematicians such as Pythagoras and his followers had developed various geometric theories. Euclid's achievement was to organize and consolidate this knowledge into a coherent framework. He built on their propositions and methods, systematizing geometry into the form we know today.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Legacy of \"Elements\"</strong><br>\nThe legacy of Euclid's \"Elements\" is monumental. It was the main textbook for teaching mathematics until the 19th century. It not only introduces geometric concepts but also includes number theory and abstract algebra, influencing the structure of mathematical education.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Euclid's Impact Beyond Mathematics</strong><br>\nEuclid's influence extends beyond mathematics into various fields, including physics, philosophy, and logic. His logical approach and method of proof have shaped the Western scientific method. Even modern education reflects Euclid's impact by teaching students to think critically and logically.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
